WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.367
-Seth Rogen. Looking good, bud.

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:03.968
Thanks for coming back to the show.

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:06.334
-Thank you for having me! Good to be here. Look at this.

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:09.501
-You know, we talk about your parents a lot on the show.

00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:11.033
-Yes.

00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:14.100
-They're both funny and nice and supportive of you.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.567
-Very supportive. -And you're -- Yeah.

00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:20.000
You're a very successful kid, so they're really --

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.467
-I am. -Yeah.

00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.501
-I am a successful kid. -Yeah, you are.

00:00:25.501 --> 00:00:28.367
But your mom -- Recently, you tweeted this out.

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:29.734
It made me laugh.

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:31.467
You said, "Did my mother just e-mail the head

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:32.968
of a major movie studio

00:00:32.968 --> 00:00:34.934
to get them to hurry up to green light our movie?

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.234
Yes. Yes, she did."

00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:39.100
[ Laughter ] Is this true?

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.567
-That is true. My mother I guess had the e-mail.

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:45.567
She asked me what I'm doing, and I had been writing a movie

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.334
with my partner, Evan, for years.

00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:49.467
And she had read the script, and she really liked it.

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:52.934
And we kept not hearing if they were going to make it.

00:00:52.934 --> 00:00:55.467
And then she called me one day and she's like,

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:58.400
"Somehow I have the e-mail of the head of the studio.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.234
I'm going to e-mail him and tell him to make your movie."

00:01:01.234 --> 00:01:04.100
And I was like, "Okay. Go for it."

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.667
Honestly, like --

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:08.300
I've always heard nepotism is a big thing in Hollywood.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.968
I've never gotten to participate in that in any way.

00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:13.300
I've worked with a lot of kids' actors --

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.033
You know, sons of famous actors, daughters.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.467
And so I thought, "Yeah, e-mail the head of the studio."

00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:19.968
And she did. And it did not work.

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:21.701
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.701 --> 00:01:23.968
-You got to try. That's such a good mom move.

00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:26.334
-So, if your mother offers to e-mail your boss,

00:01:26.334 --> 00:01:28.234
say no, I guess.

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:30.434
-Great job, Mom. Great job.

00:01:30.434 --> 00:01:31.901
Your parents must have been excited

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:33.501
that you're in a Steven Spielberg movie.

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:35.300
-They were really, really, really excited.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.767
They were very proud of me.

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.701
-What is it like to get a call from Steven Spielberg?

00:01:39.701 --> 00:01:41.667
-It was crazy. At first, I was nervous.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:43.534
I thought I was in trouble for something.

00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:46.434
I've made a lot of bad jokes over the years,

00:01:46.434 --> 00:01:50.067
probably some that he would find offensive I would imagine.

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:51.801
-You thought he was throwing you out of Hollywood?

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:53.567
-Yeah, maybe I was just getting kicked out.

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.300
-That's what happens. -That's what happens.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.434
Spielberg calls. He's like, "You're out."

00:01:57.434 --> 00:01:58.801
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:01.400
-I would get nervous, too. -I would get scared. But no.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.467
-I heard that you were the first person he thought of.

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:06.033
-I was. I was told that he had a script

00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:07.234
and he wanted me to read it.

00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:08.801
And he didn't tell me what it was about,

00:02:08.801 --> 00:02:10.434
but he was going to offer me a role in it.

00:02:10.434 --> 00:02:12.734
Which actually made me nervous, because I knew no matter

00:02:12.734 --> 00:02:15.734
what the role was, I had to take it.

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:18.367
Even if it was a terrible one. [ Laughs ]

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:20.400
And I also didn't know what kind of movie it was.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.167
I was like, "Am I going to be a dinosaur?

00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:24.267
What am I going to be exactly?"

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:26.501
I did always like that dinosaur that spits phlegm

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:28.334
'cause I am a phlegmy person.

00:02:28.334 --> 00:02:29.901
So I always thought... -Wow.

00:02:29.901 --> 00:02:31.634
-..."If there's one I can do, it's that one."

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:33.968
-I know that one.

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:38.501
-No, it was actually a deeply personal film, and I -- Yeah.

00:02:38.501 --> 00:02:42.334
And he wanted me to play someone inspired by his uncle.

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:44.567
Bernie was the real guy, and Benny is the character.

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:46.033
-Do you get nervous

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:48.667
when you're acting in front of Steven Spielberg?

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.834
-Y-- Uh, yes, a little bit.

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:54.300
But what's interesting is we shot the movie during COVID,

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.968
so he was wearing a mask

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:58.167
the entire time we were shooting.

00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:00.234
And so he didn't look like Steven Spielberg.

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:03.033
He just looked like a short Jewish guy in a mask.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.767
[ Laughter ] And if there's one thing

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:08.434
I'm very comfortable around, it's short Jewish guys.

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:10.367
That's my bread-and-butter right there.

00:03:10.367 --> 00:03:12.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:16.601
That's my [bleep]. And so --

00:03:16.601 --> 00:03:17.901
Those -- Yeah.

00:03:17.901 --> 00:03:20.934
So I wasn't that nervous when we were making the movie,

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.601
but now that we're promoting the movie,

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.100
I'm seeing him a lot without the mask,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.067
and I'm like, "Oh, my God! It's Steven Spielberg!"

00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:28.801
-"I love you!" -Yeah. "Big fan."

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:31.601
-Did you ask him for advice?

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:33.200
'Cause I know you direct, as well.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.300
-Yeah. I didn't know how he was going to direct.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.367
And I didn't know what kind of feedback he would give

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:40.067
and how that would go.

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:42.400
So, the first day, I start --

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.067
We start shooting, and we do a take.

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:47.300
And I'm like, you know -- I go back to where he's standing

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.634
and I'm, like, going to look --

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:50.267
I'm looking for feedback. -Of course.

00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:53.534
-And he is just sobbing uncontrollably.

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:56.834
And I thought, "Oh, no.

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:58.667
I'm so bad, he's crying."

00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:00.968
[ Laughter ] -This whole thing is a disaster.

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:04.033
-And he's going to fire me right now and he's going to say,

00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:06.067
"I made a mistake!"

00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:07.467
But, no.

00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:09.133
It's such a deeply personal movie

00:04:09.133 --> 00:04:10.634
that he cried a lot actually.

00:04:10.634 --> 00:04:12.667
When the scenes were happening, you would go back,

00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:14.767
and he would be weeping.

00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:16.667
I never thought it would feel great

00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:18.968
to make Steven Spielberg cry all the time.

00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:20.968
-Wow! -But it did. It felt wonderful.

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:23.000
I'd make him cry right now if I could.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.167
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:26.033
-That's so cool that it was that personal.

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:27.567
-It was really strange.

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:30.267
-And I heard he made you change your look a little bit for this.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:32.067
-A little bit, yeah.

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:37.033
The character -- The character that the character is based on

00:04:37.033 --> 00:04:40.868
had a very receding hairline, like pretty far back.

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:43.634
So I went to set a few days before I started shooting,

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:45.968
and he was like, "I want you to cut back your hairline

00:04:45.968 --> 00:04:48.934
so it looks like you're going much more bald than you are."

00:04:48.934 --> 00:04:51.601
I was like, "Okay. That's a big thing for me."

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:54.000
But how do you say no to Steven Spielberg?

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.634
So obviously I go back and I do it.

00:04:55.634 --> 00:04:57.701
And, to me, it's very drastic and I'm like, "Oh, my God.

00:04:57.701 --> 00:04:59.234
It's all anyone's going to talk about."

00:04:59.234 --> 00:05:01.534
I'm going to see everyone, and they'll be like, "Oh, my God."

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.767
And instead, I kept running into people I know

00:05:03.767 --> 00:05:07.467
and seeing them, and no one said anything.

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:10.434
And that made me realize, like, to them, I'm balding.

00:05:10.434 --> 00:05:13.067
[ Laughter ] I was already balding!

00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:15.067
It wasn't a shift.

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:19.167
I was already a balding guy, and I am a balding guy.

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:20.734
And so then I realized I'm a balding guy.

00:05:20.734 --> 00:05:22.167
So that was a big moment for me. -Okay.

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:23.567
-And then you've got to ask yourself,

00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:25.067
"Are you going to do something about it?"

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:27.200
Because a lot of actors do something about it.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.868
You have a good -- You look great.

00:05:28.868 --> 00:05:31.334
I don't know if you're doing a thing, but it's working.

00:05:31.334 --> 00:05:33.200
Whatever is going on there. -I'm not doing anything.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.634
This is real. -That is all good.

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:36.701
Because I work with a lot of actors,

00:05:36.701 --> 00:05:39.601
who, like, you know, now in their career,

00:05:39.601 --> 00:05:41.367
they have more hair than when they started,

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:42.901
which is a real thing.

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:45.267
It's a stressful double life they have...

00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:46.801
-It's too much work.

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:51.634
-...as a balding actor who is pretending to not be balding.

00:05:51.634 --> 00:05:54.367
And I don't know if I could bear that weight.

00:05:54.367 --> 00:05:56.501
But go watch the first "Avengers"

00:05:56.501 --> 00:06:00.100
and "Avengers: Endgame," they all have more hair...

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:01.567
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.567 --> 00:06:03.400
Every single one of them.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.234
[ Applause ]

00:06:06.234 --> 00:06:08.834
With a snap, their hair went...

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:10.901
50% of their hair came back.

00:06:10.901 --> 00:06:14.067
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.067 --> 00:06:17.033
-This movie is, like you said, very personal.

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:19.100
Man, it tugs at the heart strings,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:20.834
and it's getting great reviews.

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.601
Everyone top to bottom

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:23.801
is getting great reviews on this film.

00:06:23.801 --> 00:06:25.033
-Yeah. -Congratulations on this.

00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:27.501
-Thank you. It's so lovely to be a part of it.

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:29.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:29.701 --> 00:06:34.000
-Can we -- Can we set it up in any way?

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.501
-This is a scene, yeah, where Sammy is the character

00:06:37.501 --> 00:06:41.968
who is kind of representative of a young Steven Spielberg is --

00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:44.300
He's kind of debating whether or not to give up filmmaking,

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:47.467
and my character is trying to convince him to not give it up.

00:06:47.467 --> 00:06:49.067
-Here we go. I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:50.667
Here's Seth Rogen in "The Fabelmans."

00:06:50.667 --> 00:06:52.167
Take a look at this.

00:06:52.167 --> 00:06:53.901
-They're gonna change the whole goddamn world,

00:06:53.901 --> 00:06:55.934
so this was the right decision.

00:06:59.701 --> 00:07:01.534
For all sorts of reasons.

00:07:04.968 --> 00:07:08.100
Yeah, so, I'm happy for you.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.334
You know I am.

00:07:10.334 --> 00:07:13.367
But I'm going to miss you.

00:07:13.367 --> 00:07:15.367
All of you. A lot.

00:07:22.367 --> 00:07:24.534
You think whatever bad things you want about me, kiddo,

00:07:24.534 --> 00:07:28.601
but you stop making movies, it will break your mother's heart.

00:07:28.601 --> 00:07:30.801
You will break her heart. I mean it.

00:07:32.634 --> 00:07:34.334
She doesn't deserve that, not from anybody,

00:07:34.334 --> 00:07:36.300
least of all from you.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:37.734
[ Cheers and applause ] -Ah, come on.

00:07:37.734 --> 00:07:40.300
That's how you do it, buddy. Seth Rogen right there.

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:43.133
"The Fabelmans" is in select theaters now

00:07:43.133 --> 00:07:45.801
and opens in theaters everywhere November 23rd.

00:07:45.801 --> 00:07:48.968
More "Tonight Show," after the break. Stick around.

